  "number": "1.2",
  "title": "Lie Algebras",
  "body": " Lie Algebras  In this section we introduce Lie algebras. We start with understanding the matrix Lie algebras, extract their properties and then construct and analyze the formal object.   Manifolds   Topological Manifold   A topological manifold of dimension is a topological space that is Hausdorff, second-countable, and locally homeomorphic to .     Smooth Manifold   A smooth structure on a topological manifold is an equivalence class of atlases, where two atlases are equivalent if their union is a smooth atlas. A smooth manifold is a topological manifold equipped with a smooth structure.     Smooth Map   Let and be smooth manifolds. A map is smooth if for every chart on and every chart on , the map is smooth in the usual sense.     Smooth Functions   Let be a smooth manifold. A function is smooth if for every chart on , the map is smooth in the usual sense. We denote the space of all smooth functions on by .     Smooth Functions Form a Commutative Algebra   Let be a smooth manifold. Then is a commutative algebra over .      Tangent Spaces   Smooth Curve   A smooth curve in a smooth manifold is a smooth map .     Tangential Curves   Two smooth curves and in a smooth manifold are tangential at a point if for all smooth functions defined in a neighborhood of , the derivatives and are equal.  If two smooth curves and in a smooth manifold are tangential at a point , we write .     Tangential Curves is an Equivalence Relation   Let be a smooth manifold and let . Then the relation of being tangential at is an equivalence relation on the set of smooth curves in that pass through .    We denote the equivalence class of a smooth curve in a smooth manifold that passes through a point by .   Tangent Vector   A tangent vector at a point in a smooth manifold is an equivalence class of smooth curves in that are tangential at .     Tangent Space   The tangent space of a smooth manifold at a point , denoted , is the set of equivalence classes of smooth curves in that are tangential at .     Tangent Space is a Vector Space   If is a smooth manifold and , then the tangent space is a vector space over .     Tangent Bundle   The tangent bundle of a smooth manifold , denoted , is the disjoint union of all tangent spaces at each point in . In symbols, . There is a natural projection map that sends each tangent vector to the point in at which it is tangent.      Differential Maps   Differential Map   If is a smooth map of smooth manifolds and , then the maps defined by is called the differential of at .     Differential Map is Well Defined   If is a smooth map of smooth manifolds and , then the differential map is well defined.    In the case where is a smooth function on and , then the differential map if precisely the standard single variable derivative In this case, we call the directional derivative of at .   Differential Map is Linear   If is a smooth map of smooth manifolds and , then the differential map is linear.      Vector Fields  Let be a smooth manifold. Let denote the space of smooth functions on . A vector field on is a smooth assignment of a tangent vector to each point of . In other words, a vector field is a smooth map such that , where is the canonical projection. The space of all vector fields on is denoted by .   Vector Field   If is a smooth manifold, then a vector field on is a smooth assignment of a tangent vector to each point of . More precisely, if is the tangent bundle, then a vector field is a smooth map such that .     Directional Derivative   If is a smooth manifold and , then the directional derivative  is a linear map from to defined by     By abuse of notation, we will often write instead of for the directional derivative associated to .   Directional Derivative Satisfy Leibniz Rule   If , then the directional derivative satisfies the Leibniz rule: for all .     Hint  This is a direct proof. You may find and and useful.     Derivation   A derivation on a commutative algebra is a linear map that satisfies the Leibniz rule: for all . Let denote the space of all derivations on .     Vector Field and Derivations are Isomorphic   If is a smooth manifold, then the map defined by is an isomorphism of vector spaces.        Lie Bracket of Derivations   If is a smooth manifold and , then the Lie bracket of and is the map defined by defined by for all .     The Lie Bracket of Derivations is a Derivation   If is a smooth manifold and , then the Lie bracket is also a derivation.    This is a direct proof following definitions. Let . First check Expanding this expression and using the Leibniz rule for and , we obtain Simplifying, we find that Thus the Lie bracket satisfies the Leibniz rule, and hence is a derivation.     The Lie Bracket of Derivations Satisfies the Jacobi Identity   If is a smooth manifold and , then the Lie bracket This is known as the Jacobi identity .    This is a direct proof following definitions. We expand each term, with adjacent derivations denoting composition. Adding these three expressions, we find that all terms cancel, and we obtain the Jacobi identity.    Via the isomorphism , we can define a Lie bracket on the space of vector fields by defining for all . In other words, the Lie bracket of vector fields is defined by the commutator of the associated directional derivatives. In the remainder of the book, as is common in the field, we will omit the isomorphism and just often write for the Lie bracket of vector fields.   Lie Algebra   A vector space is a Lie algebra if it is equipped with a bilinear map called the Lie bracket that satisfies the following properties:   Antisymmetry : for all .   Jacobi Identity : for all .        If is a smooth manifold, then the space of vector fields is a Lie algebra with respect to the Lie bracket defined by for all and .     Lie Algebra Homomorphism   A linear map between Lie algebras is a Lie algebra homomorphism if it preserves the Lie bracket, i.e., for all .     -Related Vector Fields   If is a smooth map between smooth manifolds, and and are vector fields, we say that and are -related if for all , we have       If is a smooth map between smooth manifolds and with and and are vector fields such that and are -related and and are -related, then and are -related.         Left-Invariant Vector Fields  We now return to Lie groups.   Left Invariant Vector Field   A left invariant vector field on a Lie group is a vector field such that for all , we have where is the left translation by .      If is a Lie group, then the set of left invariant vector fields on is a Lie subalgebra of under the Lie bracket of vector fields.     Lie Algebra of a Lie Group   The Lie algebra of a Lie group , which we will denote by , is the set of left-invariant vector fields on , equipped with a bracket operation [ , ] that makes it a Lie algebra.      The Lie algebra of a Lie group is isomorphic as a vector space to the tangent space at the identity element .      If is a Lie group homomorphism, then is a Lie algebra homomorphism.     Lie algebra isomorphism implies local diffeomorphism   If is a Lie group homomorphism and is an isomorphism, then is a local diffeomorphism.     "
